WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.137
- Good morning Believe Nation.

00:00:01.137 --> 00:00:02.472
My name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:02.472 --> 00:00:05.359
My one word is 'believe' and
I believe that entrepreneurs

00:00:05.359 --> 00:00:08.082
will solve all of the
world's major problems.

00:00:08.082 --> 00:00:10.653
So to help you on your
journey today's message is,

00:00:10.653 --> 00:00:12.653
"Go work at McDonald's".

00:00:13.589 --> 00:00:16.144
Over to you Robert Kiyosaki.

00:00:16.144 --> 00:00:20.500
(rooster crows) "♫ I
wake up every morning."

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.380
"♫ Espresso keep me going."

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:27.142
"♫ I wake up every morning."

00:00:27.142 --> 00:00:29.567
- You wake up tomorrow morning

00:00:29.567 --> 00:00:33.475
and you're on the street
and you have nothing.

00:00:33.475 --> 00:00:37.475
You don't have a single
contact in your network.

00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:42.217
All you have is the knowledge and

00:00:43.218 --> 00:00:45.809
experience that you've had
throughout your career.

00:00:45.809 --> 00:00:47.918
How do you build up from there?

00:00:47.918 --> 00:00:50.449
- Well, I've been there, many times.

00:00:50.449 --> 00:00:51.885
So, it's no big deal.

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:53.061
You just figure out what's needed

00:00:53.061 --> 00:00:55.884
and wanted and you go do it.

00:00:55.884 --> 00:01:00.206
People always kind of dump
on, what do you call it?

00:01:00.206 --> 00:01:02.500
Fast food industry.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.878
If I had to do it again,
I'd go work for McDonald's.

00:01:05.878 --> 00:01:08.833
The reason I'd go work for
McDonald's, whether it's seven

00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:11.447
bucks an hour or 50, I don't really care;

00:01:11.447 --> 00:01:13.710
I don't really like
McDonald's food but they have

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:16.257
the best business systems in the world.

00:01:16.257 --> 00:01:20.354
How else can a company take
virtually non-college educated

00:01:20.354 --> 00:01:25.115
people and build a fast food
industry across the world?

00:01:25.115 --> 00:01:26.466
And they have the best system,

00:01:26.466 --> 00:01:28.805
so right inside that little
McDonald's you can learn

00:01:28.805 --> 00:01:30.882
everything you need to
learn about business

00:01:30.882 --> 00:01:33.472
except for the management side of it.

00:01:33.472 --> 00:01:35.192
So, I'd just go work at McDonald's

00:01:35.192 --> 00:01:37.019
so I could learn even more.

00:01:37.019 --> 00:01:39.269
I look at life as learning.

00:01:40.126 --> 00:01:41.690
I'm constantly learning.

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:45.717
You know, I'm reading
constantly, studying constantly.

00:01:45.717 --> 00:01:49.086
I spend most of my time
with entrepreneurs.

00:01:49.086 --> 00:01:50.731
I don't spend my time with people who

00:01:50.731 --> 00:01:53.669
complain about the economy, you know.

00:01:53.669 --> 00:01:56.253
So, nothing would really change.

00:01:56.253 --> 00:01:58.960
I'd just go back to being an entrepreneur.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:00.350
- If you want to learn
how to do something,

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:01.970
the fastest way to do it is to go work

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:04.114
for somebody who's already done it.

00:02:04.114 --> 00:02:06.294
Now, I'm not as huge a
fan about working for

00:02:06.294 --> 00:02:10.057
a giant corporation because
often you are assigned

00:02:10.057 --> 00:02:12.842
a really specific task and
you don't really get to

00:02:12.842 --> 00:02:15.902
explore quickly but if you
work for somebody who's

00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:18.858
got a much smaller
company, an entrepreneur,

00:02:18.858 --> 00:02:20.864
then you get to take on a
lot more responsibilities.

00:02:20.864 --> 00:02:23.004
My idea is I would suggest
to go work for somebody

00:02:23.004 --> 00:02:25.550
who has 10 employees or
less, who's doing exactly

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:27.766
what you want to do, and get as close to

00:02:27.766 --> 00:02:30.009
the entrepreneur founder as possible.

00:02:30.009 --> 00:02:33.330
You get hired to whatever job
it is that you want to learn.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:34.163
Right?

00:02:34.163 --> 00:02:36.194
But then you get as close to
the entrepreneur as possible,

00:02:36.194 --> 00:02:38.999
take on more work, work
overtime, work extra hours

00:02:38.999 --> 00:02:41.892
because this isn't work;
this is your education.

00:02:41.892 --> 00:02:44.028
You're getting paid to
learn instead of paying

00:02:44.028 --> 00:02:47.474
to go to a university you
are getting paid to make

00:02:47.474 --> 00:02:50.187
mistakes, to learn how things
are done, to understanding

00:02:50.187 --> 00:02:54.919
if this is even an industry
that you want to get into.

00:02:54.919 --> 00:02:57.611
You're getting paid to learn.

00:02:57.611 --> 00:02:59.364
And that's the best way
to go out and do it.

00:02:59.364 --> 00:03:02.641
So, when I sold my first
business, as an example,

00:03:02.641 --> 00:03:05.000
I wanted to learn about raising capital.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:06.340
I wanted to learn that process.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:07.937
I wanted to learn from
people who'd gone through it.

00:03:07.937 --> 00:03:09.636
I didn't have to raise
money for my business;

00:03:09.636 --> 00:03:11.937
we thought about it, we almost
did it but we got bought

00:03:11.937 --> 00:03:14.272
before we needed to go and do it.

00:03:14.272 --> 00:03:15.919
So after I sold my
company, I went to go work

00:03:15.919 --> 00:03:19.789
at a venture capital company
and I connected with these VCs.

00:03:19.789 --> 00:03:21.713
There were four of them
in their 60s or 70s.

00:03:21.713 --> 00:03:24.657
They knew the street, they
had a wealth of experience

00:03:24.657 --> 00:03:28.873
and education, and I helped
them bring in new leads.

00:03:28.873 --> 00:03:32.091
In that process, I learned not just about

00:03:32.091 --> 00:03:36.001
the raising capital industry,
the game, how it works,

00:03:36.001 --> 00:03:39.176
what people look for, what it
all looks like because I had

00:03:39.176 --> 00:03:42.122
zero concept, I never
needed it from my business;

00:03:42.122 --> 00:03:43.913
but I also got to see how they operated,

00:03:43.913 --> 00:03:45.448
how they thought about business,

00:03:45.448 --> 00:03:47.301
the advice that they
gave the entrepreneur.

00:03:47.301 --> 00:03:50.481
One of my favorite
experiences was we got hired

00:03:50.481 --> 00:03:54.648
to basically be a temporary
chairman for a company,

00:03:55.903 --> 00:03:59.390
to go in and advise the CEO of what to do.

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:01.363
Every month we'd go in
and look at the numbers

00:04:01.363 --> 00:04:03.422
and have conversations and guide the CEO

00:04:03.422 --> 00:04:05.444
to make better decisions in her business.

00:04:05.444 --> 00:04:06.591
It was awesome.

00:04:06.591 --> 00:04:09.340
It was awesome to see
how these guys thought.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:13.100
A lot of what they learned
and taught I picked up,

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:15.861
right as the guys were doing
what I wanted to learn.

00:04:15.861 --> 00:04:18.141
So it's the fastest way to learn.

00:04:18.141 --> 00:04:20.589
Whatever it is that you want
to do, if you're uncertain,

00:04:20.589 --> 00:04:23.235
if you need more education,
if you haven't committed

00:04:23.235 --> 00:04:26.502
to getting going yet; go work
for somebody who's done it.

00:04:26.502 --> 00:04:27.499
Model their success.

00:04:27.499 --> 00:04:29.199
Again, first they'll
teach you if this is even

00:04:29.199 --> 00:04:30.727
an industry you want to get into.

00:04:30.727 --> 00:04:32.123
Sometimes you get into
something and you realize,

00:04:32.123 --> 00:04:34.413
"you know what, I don't really like that,

00:04:34.413 --> 00:04:36.154
I don't really want to do that" and better

00:04:36.154 --> 00:04:37.322
to figure it out while working

00:04:37.322 --> 00:04:38.611
for somebody else than to invest

00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:41.326
your own time, money,
and resources into it.

00:04:41.326 --> 00:04:43.858
And two, they'll shake out your path.

00:04:43.858 --> 00:04:45.800
Instead of you failing and
struggling over and over

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:47.960
and over and over again, they'll teach you

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.162
how to do it a lot more quickly.

00:04:50.162 --> 00:04:52.058
And then I'd also start
my thing on the side.

00:04:52.058 --> 00:04:54.146
I'd be moonlighting, I'd
be working part-time,

00:04:54.146 --> 00:04:55.353
I'd be finding a way to get my own

00:04:55.353 --> 00:04:57.234
business up on the side.

00:04:57.234 --> 00:04:59.182
But learning from somebody
who's already done

00:04:59.182 --> 00:05:01.135
exactly what you want to do, I believe,

00:05:01.135 --> 00:05:04.235
is one of the fastest ways to
get to where you want to go.

00:05:04.235 --> 00:05:07.065
So the question made today
is I'm curious what is

00:05:07.065 --> 00:05:09.442
one of the greatest lessons
that you have learned

00:05:09.442 --> 00:05:12.044
from somebody else that
you've applied to your life

00:05:12.044 --> 00:05:15.220
or to your business recently,
like in the past year?

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:16.958
The greatest lesson you've
learned in the past year

00:05:16.958 --> 00:05:19.230
from somebody that's had
a big impact in your life

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.509
or your business leave it
down in the comments below.

00:05:21.509 --> 00:05:22.676
I'm really curious to find out.

00:05:22.676 --> 00:05:24.892
I also want to give a quick
shout out to June Thomas.

00:05:24.892 --> 00:05:26.462
June, thank you so much for picking up

00:05:26.462 --> 00:05:28.225
a copy of my book "Your One Word".

00:05:28.225 --> 00:05:29.893
I really really appreciate the support

00:05:29.893 --> 00:05:32.154
and I hope you enjoyed the read.

00:05:32.154 --> 00:05:33.720
So thank you guys so much for watching.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:34.824
I believe in you.

00:05:34.824 --> 00:05:36.755
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:05:36.755 --> 00:05:39.075
and remember; your one word is?

00:05:39.075 --> 00:05:41.462
Much love and I'll see
you again tomorrow morning

00:05:41.462 --> 00:05:44.296
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:05:44.296 --> 00:05:47.108
"♫ I wake up every morning."

00:05:47.108 --> 00:05:50.626
"♫ Espresso keeps me going."

00:05:50.626 --> 00:05:53.431
(typing)

00:05:53.431 --> 00:05:56.605
- [Charles] When you cease
to learn, you cease to grow.

00:05:56.605 --> 00:05:58.590
When you cease to learn, you sort of

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.769
begin to decay a little bit.

00:06:00.769 --> 00:06:03.852
You are to learn how to stay healthy.

00:06:05.088 --> 00:06:06.904
And you say, "Well, how do I do that?"

00:06:06.904 --> 00:06:10.036
Well, the proper diet,
and exercise, and rest.

00:06:10.036 --> 00:06:11.370
That's part of it.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:14.945
In other words, we're
not healthy by accident.

00:06:14.945 --> 00:06:17.438
It's by learning some key things in life

00:06:17.438 --> 00:06:20.499
that really make a difference.

00:06:20.499 --> 00:06:23.615
When we keep learning
something happens in our life.

00:06:23.615 --> 00:06:25.647
For example, you and
I are living in an age

00:06:25.647 --> 00:06:28.107
when we've had to keep learning.

00:06:28.107 --> 00:06:29.903
Because when I was born there were

00:06:29.903 --> 00:06:32.903
no computers that I knew of in 1932.

00:06:35.265 --> 00:06:37.719
We go back and you look at your life

00:06:37.719 --> 00:06:41.631
and think about all the things
that you've had to learn

00:06:41.631 --> 00:06:45.648
that your predecessors
and your parents didn't

00:06:45.648 --> 00:06:48.757
have to learn because
they didn't even exist.

00:06:48.757 --> 00:06:51.233
If you're going to be useful
all the days of your life,

00:06:51.233 --> 00:06:54.848
you've got to be willing to keep learning.

00:06:54.848 --> 00:06:59.100
I would simply say that a
lazy brain is an aging brain.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:00.457
You don't want to learn?

00:07:00.457 --> 00:07:03.548
What happens; you start decaying.

00:07:03.548 --> 00:07:07.029
There's something interesting and exciting

00:07:07.029 --> 00:07:09.170
about learning things in life.

00:07:09.170 --> 00:07:12.349
No matter who you are and no
matter what your age might be.

00:07:12.349 --> 00:07:15.389
- Sometimes when it's all
about you, like I said,

00:07:15.389 --> 00:07:17.343
never get high on your own supply, right?

00:07:17.343 --> 00:07:21.510
When it's all about you, you
know like Biggie says actually,

00:07:22.836 --> 00:07:25.909
or actually Scarface,
we'll go back all the way

00:07:25.909 --> 00:07:28.576
to the original reference, but,

00:07:30.552 --> 00:07:34.365
you can't think that you're
the only game in town.

00:07:34.365 --> 00:07:37.446
That's a really dangerous
thought process, man.

00:07:37.446 --> 00:07:40.209
Collaboration's good and it's healthy.

00:07:40.209 --> 00:07:42.658
Working with young cats that are coming up

00:07:42.658 --> 00:07:46.116
and have their own revenge fantasies?

00:07:46.116 --> 00:07:47.821
Yeah, you could learn from that.

00:07:47.821 --> 00:07:49.163
I'm not too old to learn, man.

00:07:49.163 --> 00:07:51.863
I've been going to school
since; I might not have been

00:07:51.863 --> 00:07:54.472
in the classroom but
school has come to me.

00:07:54.472 --> 00:07:56.869
Like when you want to
learn, school comes to you.

00:07:56.869 --> 00:07:58.591
- What is like the
number one thing to take

00:07:58.591 --> 00:08:02.273
from the "Ego is the Enemy"
and a piece of advice

00:08:02.273 --> 00:08:06.167
you would give to a young
Ryan Holiday who's now

00:08:06.167 --> 00:08:09.224
19, 20 years old that's
watching this right now?

00:08:09.224 --> 00:08:11.495
- Yeah so one of my favorite
sections of the book

00:08:11.495 --> 00:08:15.962
is this idea that you can
never stop being a student.

00:08:15.962 --> 00:08:17.698
Because students are humble
because they understand

00:08:17.698 --> 00:08:20.308
that there's someone above
them who can teach them.

00:08:20.308 --> 00:08:22.597
So you never stop being a student.

00:08:22.597 --> 00:08:25.642
There's a quote from Epictetus that says,

00:08:25.642 --> 00:08:29.170
"One cannot learn that which
they think they already know."

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:31.474
And so, if you think you
already know everything,

00:08:31.474 --> 00:08:32.780
you've stopped being a student

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:35.198
and you're not going to learn anymore.

00:08:35.198 --> 00:08:36.535
In some ways it's over for you.

00:08:36.535 --> 00:08:38.728
I was forced to look at the world

00:08:38.728 --> 00:08:39.824
the way he looks at the world.

00:08:39.824 --> 00:08:43.363
Like how I researched books I
learned from Robert's system,

00:08:43.363 --> 00:08:45.645
because as his research
assistant he wasn't like,

00:08:45.645 --> 00:08:47.218
"Oh, go make up your own
system;" he was like,

00:08:47.218 --> 00:08:49.483
"This is how I do it, you
need to do it this way

00:08:49.483 --> 00:08:50.762
so you can be of service to me."

00:08:50.762 --> 00:08:53.461
And so you're sort of
learning how to do that thing

00:08:53.461 --> 00:08:55.884
rather than like sitting
down and reading a book

00:08:55.884 --> 00:08:58.599
about it or trying to learn a song by ear.

00:08:58.599 --> 00:09:01.903
It's like no, somebody showed
you how to do each step.

